
- Precise manifesto pledges give the electorate a clear set of issues and policies that the government will be committed to pursuing
- Manifestos are therefore an attempt to convince voters that it is in their rational interest or that the issue they value most will be delivered
- By delivering on manifesto commitments, a party can claim to have delivered what they had promised to and therefore appeal to valence voting
- Essentially, voters must make a judgement on the collection of policies offered in a manifesto to help determine whom to award with an electoral mandate
- Manifestos also establish mandate; i.e. the consent of the people, allowing a political party to do what it feels is necessary in the national interest if it succeeds in being elected to government
- When [[Parties]] win an election and forms the government it has a mandate to carry out all the policy commitments contained in its election manifesto
- The concept of the [[mandate]] if fundamental to democracy: it is how permission is granted from the people to the government
- The [[mandate]] also gives the winning party the opportunity to set out its legislative agenda in Parliament, based on the manifestos promises it made
